[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

How would you like to approach this? Feel free to take a moment to gather your thoughts first.

[candidate silent 9s]

[candidate · 1:39] Let me lay out how I'd structure this. Since revenue is actually up 15% and margin fell from 24% to 6%, the story is almost certainly on the cost side, so I'd start there and only sanity-check revenue.

On revenue, I'd want to separate price from volume — I'll assume for now that the growth came from more stores or more transactions rather than price increases, since a price-led growth would usually protect margin.

On costs, I'd split fixed versus variable: rent and occupancy, labor, coffee beans and dairy inputs, equipment and depreciation, then corporate overhead and marketing.

The useful thing here would be a breakdown of costs as a percent of revenue for both years, so I can see which line expanded. I'd also want to know whether the store count changed over those two years, because new stores can drag margin while they ramp.

Does that structure work as a starting point?
