[interviewer · ANALYSIS · 3:16] Understood. The cost breakdown you asked for is below. What stands out to you? COGS is 58% of revenue today, up from 42% two years ago. Labor is 22% of revenue, and it’s been stable. Overhead is 14% of revenue, up slightly from 12%. To your question on the data: Total revenue is $480M a year. I'll come to that data shortly.

[candidate · 4:46] COGS is the whole story. Let me check the arithmetic: today 58 + 22 + 14 = 94, leaving 6% — matches. Two years ago 42 + 22 + 12 = 76, leaving 24% — also matches. So of the 18 points lost, 16 come from COGS and 2 from overhead.

On $480M of revenue, that 16-point swing is about $76.8M a year. That's the prize.

Now, COGS rising as a percent of revenue means either input costs per unit went up, or prices didn't move with them, or the mix shifted toward lower-margin items. I'll assume for the moment that prices have been held flat, because a chain that passed costs through wouldn't see the ratio move this way.

What I'd really like is the COGS line broken into its components — beans, dairy, packaging, food — for both years, so I can see whether this is one input or everything at once.
