[interviewer · EXHIBIT · 4:50] The arithmetic on the margin bridge ties to the cost figures. I'll come back to the dollar sizing. Menu prices have not changed in two years. Coffee beans were 25% of COGS two years ago. Raw coffee bean costs are up 40% over the past two years. Other input costs — dairy, packaging, and food — are up 37.5% over the past two years.

[candidate · 6:20] Let me run it. Old COGS was 42% of revenue. Beans at 25% of that is 10.5 points of revenue; everything else is 31.5 points.

Beans up 40%: 10.5 × 1.4 = 14.7 points.
Other inputs up 37.5%: 31.5 × 1.375 = 43.3 points.
Total: 58.0 points. That reconciles exactly with the 58% you gave, so no volume or mix effect is hiding in there — it's pure input inflation with prices frozen.

What's striking is that beans only explain 4.2 of the 16 points. The other 11.8 come from dairy, packaging and food, because that bucket is three times larger even though the inflation rate is similar. So the instinct to blame coffee prices would misdiagnose this — it's broad-based commodity inflation.

The obvious lever is price, since menu prices haven't moved in two years while inputs ran up nearly 40%. I'd want a sense of how price-sensitive the customer base is before sizing that.
