[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 7:55] I don't have competitive price points or any loyalty or location-level cut. Beyond price, what else could Brew & Bean do to recover margin?

[candidate · 9:25] Fine — I'll work without it and flag elasticity as the key risk.

Three other levers.

Procurement. Inputs are 58 points of revenue, so even modest sourcing wins are large. With 200 stores there should be scale: consolidate vendors, lock in forward contracts or hedges on beans and dairy rather than buying spot, and renegotiate packaging, which is usually the least differentiated line and the easiest to re-bid.

Mix and product. Shift the push toward high-margin items — brewed coffee, espresso drinks without heavy dairy — and reformulate or reprice the dairy-intensive and food items that are dragging. Cheaper substitutes on packaging, smaller cup sizes at the same price, that kind of thing.

Waste and portioning. Specialty coffee typically runs real spoilage on milk and pastries. Tightening par levels and portion control attacks COGS without touching the customer price.

Overhead is the smaller piece — 2 points, about $9.6M — but it's worth asking what grew there, since revenue rose and it should have leveraged down.

What about last year, as a midpoint? It would tell us whether this is still worsening.
