[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 9:29] I don't have a midpoint year. The data covers today and two years ago. Given the levers you've laid out, what's your recommendation to the CEO?

[candidate · 10:59] My recommendation: Brew & Bean should put through a staged menu price increase of roughly 10–12%, paired with a procurement reset, and target recovering about 10 to 12 margin points over the next year.

The reasoning. This isn't an operations problem — labor held at 22% and overhead moved only 2 points. Sixteen of the 18 points lost are input inflation that was never passed to the customer, while menu prices sat flat for two years. On $480M, that's roughly $77M of profit sitting on the table.

Price is the fastest lever and the cushion is wide: at a 10.6% increase we could lose about 20% of volume and still break even. Procurement is the durable lever — forward contracts on beans and dairy, re-bid packaging — and it's underexploited at 200 stores.

The main risk is elasticity, which I couldn't test without competitive pricing. So I'd stage it: pilot a 10% increase in a subset of markets, measure traffic for 8–12 weeks, then roll out. Simultaneously, audit waste and run the vendor re-bid, which carry no customer risk.
